From fd3540d2df3f8840a486c1f892fe87bf035b3b79 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: yihou <yihou@amd.com>
Date: Sat, 1 Aug 2026 05:55:54 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] feat(engine): make the sglang readiness timeout
 configurable

The hardcoded 30 min covers weight load plus cuda-graph capture for the models
we ship recipes for, but not a 700 GiB checkpoint read twice -- MTP re-reads the
weights to extract the draft layer -- and least of all when both PD legs load
off the same filesystem at once. That leg gets killed while it is still making
progress, so the whole bring-up has to start over.

Read `$INFERA_SGLANG_READY_TIMEOUT` when no explicit timeout is passed, the same
shape as the existing `INFERA_ATOM_READY_TIMEOUT`.

Cherry-picked from liyingli's PR #56 (0bb23c7). The env-reference row lands in
the next commit, which adds a second row to the same new table.

Liying's commit ships no test; added one. It asserts the resolved value via the
`TimeoutError` message rather than by waiting, driving the timeout branch with a
clock already past the deadline, so the 1800 s default is checked in
milliseconds. It also covers the two ways a misconfiguration could silently
disable the guard: an empty value must mean "unset", not "0 seconds", and a
non-numeric value must raise rather than be coerced.

Written with `asyncio.run` in sync test functions rather than
`@pytest.mark.asyncio` on purpose: the engine image ships pytest without
pytest-asyncio, where that marker is an unknown mark and every async test is
silently collected, never awaited, and reported as passing. Verified executing
in the built image on chi2879: 5 passed.

Co-authored-by: liyingli <liyingli@amd.com>
Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
